by nicolas-grekas at 2026-07-26T15:50:06Z

Reviewed, rebased onto the current 8.2 and reworked. Thanks @ker0x, and sorry this sat so long.

I changed the mechanism from `row_attr` to a dedicated `form_id` view variable. Three problems came from overloading `row_attr`, and all three disappear with a variable of its own:

* `row_attr` already means the attributes of a field's row wrapper. Rendering it on the `<form>` tag makes an existing `row_attr`, for instance one set by a `FormTypeExtension` to class every row, start appearing on the form element too.
* `form_start` rendered `row_attr|merge(attr)`, and `merge` lets the right operand win, so `attr` overrode `row_attr`. A bootstrap 3 horizontal form with `attr: {class: "app-form"}` therefore lost the theme's own `form-horizontal` class: `class="app-form form-horizontal"` became `class="app-form"`. @xabbuh asked for a fallback to `attr` for themes that do not provide `row_attr`, and this is the half that was missing. The coverage that would have caught it had been converted from `attr` to `row_attr`, so nothing passed `attr` to `renderStart` any more.
* the id was computed twice, once in `FormType` for the tag and once in `BaseType` for the children's `form` attribute. Overriding `row_attr` at render time, which the PR's own updated test documents as the way to add a class, left `<form>` with the app's id and the children pointing at `form_my_form`, that is at nothing.

I also kept the `form_attr` string form as it is. The earlier version made it stop setting `vars["id"]` for the root, which changed every descendant id and every `label for=`. That is a wider break than the one the UPGRADE entry described, and it is not needed for the fix.

What is left is a `form_id` variable on root forms, `form_div_layout` filling `attr.id` from it when the application has not set one, and `BaseType` pointing `form_attr` children at the same value. Since every theme inherits `form_start` from `form_div_layout`, none of the other layouts needed a change, and `bootstrap_3_horizontal_layout.html.twig` is untouched.

Tests now prove the fix rather than record the output: the `<form>` id and the children's `form` attribute are asserted equal, an explicit `attr.id` is asserted to win, and the `form_attr` string form is asserted not to move descendant ids. The first fails on a clean 8.2. The start-tag expectations are updated across all layouts.

One open detail: there is no way to suppress the id. `attr: {id: ""}` renders `id=""`, which is invalid HTML. I did not add an opt-out; say if you want one.

Local runs: Form 4939, Twig bridge 2002, both green. php-cs-fixer clean.

